It gets a lot funkier than that: If you get hit for 15 hp of damage,
it's of little importance whether your damage reduction roll ends up
16 or 40 - you'll always receive minimum damage; getting a higher
"maximum roll" just adds a couple more chances of getting tagged for
minimum damage (dunno whether that's zero or one). And improving
negative AC also affects the chance of being hit - the AC a monster
has to hit is rolled between zero and your nominal AC for every
attack; going from -42 AC to -48 AC adds quite a bunch of "sure
misses".

I assumed an attack doing 20HP of average damage before damage
reduction rolls and hitting a -42 AC character half the time (and
never connects if the AC roll ends up -40 or better). If i'm anywhere
near correct, the actual damage reduction caused by improving AC to
-48 is barely more than half a hitpoint on average, but that
translates to about *20%* less damage received. The effects will be
weaker for attacks which do less damage and/or hit less frequently.

